Here are the main problems with that handler, grouped by type, plus concrete suggestions for fixing each:

1. Leaking sensitive data  
   • You are selecting `*` and then returning the user’s password back to the client in plain text.  
   • Even if you weren’t returning the password, fetching every column (created_at, deleted_at, metadata, etc.) and passing it straight through is a bad habit.  

  Fixes:  
  – In your SQL only pull back the columns you actually need:  
      const { rows } = await db.query(
        'SELECT id, username, email, created_at FROM users WHERE id = $1',
        [userId]
      )  
  – Or, if you really need to fetch `*`, strip out the password before you send anything to the client:  
      const user = rows[0]  
      delete user.password  
      res.json(user)  

2. No error handling / unhandled promise rejections  
   • If the database is down or you make a typo in your SQL, this code will throw and your server will crash (or at best return a 500 without a helpful message).  

  Fixes:  
  – Wrap your async handler in try/catch (or use a helper like `express-async-handler`):  
      app.get('/api/users/:id', asyncHandler(async (req, res) => {  
        // …  
      }))  
  – Inside the catch, log the error and return a 500 with a generic message.  

3. No “not found” handling  
   • If `db.query` returns zero rows, `user.rows[0]` is undefined and you’ll end up with a hard-to-read 500 or even a crash.  

  Fix:  
      if (!user.rows.length) {  
        return res.status(404).json({ error: 'User not found' });  
      }  

4. No input validation  
   • `req.params.id` can be anything—`"../../../etc/passwd"`, a long string, `NaN`, etc.  Even though you’re using a parameterized query (so you’ve largely eliminated SQL injection), you still want to ensure it’s of the correct shape/type.  

  Fix:  
      const userId = parseInt(req.params.id, 10);  
      if (isNaN(userId)) {  
        return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Invalid user ID' });  
      }  

5. No authorization check  
   • As written, anyone who can hit your endpoint can fetch anyone else’s profile.  Unless this is a public user profile (and you’ve scrubbed out truly private fields), you should check that the caller is allowed to see that record.  

  Fixes (examples):  
  – If you’re using JWTs or sessions, extract `req.user.id` and compare it to `req.params.id` (or check `req.user.role === 'admin'`).  
  – Otherwise, return 403 for unauthorized access.  

Putting it all together, a more robust version might look like:

```
const asyncHandler = require('express-async-handler');

app.get(
  '/api/users/:id',
  authenticate,       // e.g. populates req.user
  asyncHandler(async (req, res) => {
    // 1) Validate & normalize
    const userId = parseInt(req.params.id, 10);
    if (isNaN(userId)) {
      return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Invalid user ID' });
    }

    // 2) Authorization: only self or admin
    if (req.user.id !== userId && req.user.role !== 'admin') {
      return res.status(403).json({ error: 'Forbidden' });
    }

    // 3) Fetch only required columns
    const { rows } = await db.query(
      'SELECT id, username, email, created_at FROM users WHERE id = $1',
      [userId]
    );

    // 4) Handle not found
    if (!rows.length) {
      return res.status(404).json({ error: 'User not found' });
    }

    // 5) Send safe data
    res.json(rows[0]);
  })
);
```

Summary of fixes:
• Don’t SELECT * or return passwords  
• Proper try/catch or async-handler  
• Return 404 if no rows  
• Validate and sanitize `req.params.id`  
• Enforce authorization so users can’t enumerate or spy on each other
